{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789949625,"nonce":"DqylDClwW9ZngCeNgn0S/jK2nR8=","title":"A function used as a value returned a box where its type said Int; untyped parameters were boxed pointers doing arithmetic","message":"Found by self-checking probes of ordinary Haxe (scratchpad/probe/S3, D2, TP1-5).\n\n1. `apply(f:Int-\u003eInt-\u003eInt, x, y) return f(x, y)` with `apply(compare, 5, 2)`\n   answered 38639152064: the closure-value adapter (5e40a9ed) spoke an\n   erased `(env, i64..) -\u003e i64` ABI that unboxed its arguments heuristically\n   and BOXED its scalar result, while a lambda of the same type is compiled\n   as `(env, declared..) -\u003e declared` and every typed call site reads the\n   declared result. Only a Dynamic-typed call site ever read a box. The\n   adapter now has the lambda's shape: it drops the env and forwards.\n   `arr.sort(compare)`, `ArraySort.sort(arr, compare)`, `var f = compare;\n   f(5, 2)` all return the right values now.\n\n2. Issue6480's IIFE `(function(f, o) {...})(blub, {counter: 0})` was the one\n   consumer of the boxed convention: `f` was Dynamic. A function literal\n   called in place now types its unannotated parameters from the arguments.\n\n3. Every unannotated parameter was Dynamic, and the body did pointer\n   arithmetic on the boxes: `sub(from, to) return to - from` gave 96,\n   `lt(4, 1)` true, `a[from]` 0. `param_types_from_uses` now reads the\n   operator uses the way Haxe unifies the monomorph: arithmetic, comparison,\n   bit ops, `++`/`-x` and an array index make it Int; a condition or `!p`\n   Bool; `var x:T = p` and `return p` under an annotated return take T; a\n   local declared as a plain copy (`var i = from`) carries its uses; `\"\" + p`\n   is String only when nothing else constrains it; a literal default types\n   the parameter (`b = 4.25` was Dynamic and printed as the f64 bits). This\n   also typed ArraySort's `rec(a, cmp, from, to)`, which had made Issue6722\n   nondeterministic (543e595).\n\n4. A lambda returned from a `:Void-\u003eDynamic` function returned its scalar\n   raw (`return function() { return i; }`), `cast e` to Dynamic\n   reinterpreted a scalar, a Dynamic assigned or returned as a function type\n   stayed boxed, and a Dynamic condition tested its box's address. The\n   literal takes the enclosing function's declared Dynamic result (a\n   formal's function type is NOT consulted: an unresolved type parameter\n   there reads as Dynamic too, and Issue2378 boxed a String into an\n   `Array\u003cS\u003e`), a lambda body typed Dynamic boxes against its own result\n   (other lambdas keep the enclosing context on purpose: a `Null\u003cInt\u003e`\n   result would box what parseInt's callers read raw, Issue2874), an unsafe\n   cast to Dynamic boxes -- with a class, interface, type-parameter or\n   abstract-underlying context adopted as the target instead, since\n   `this.value = cast v` and `return cast value` in a `@:from` were casts to\n   T and to String, not to Dynamic (Issue2835, Issue6656) -- a float into an\n   erased slot keeps its bits, `haxe_unbox_if_tag` opens a function box at\n   a Let or return into a function type, and `haxe_dynamic_truthy` reads a\n   Dynamic condition.\n\n   The recurring hazard behind every one of the reverts above: a `Dynamic`\n   in a type is as often a DECAY (unannotated parameter, unresolved type\n   parameter, forward-declared return) as a declaration, and a boxing rule\n   keyed on the declared type alone boxes a value its consumer reads raw.\n\nOpen, same family:\n- A call through a Dynamic-typed function value (`applyDyn(f, x, y) return\n  f(x, y)`) passes raw arguments and reads a raw result; both sides need a\n  convention the callee can be found under. Design: register an erased\n  entry per closure target at module init and dispatch Dynamic calls\n  through it (see the dynamic-entry notes in the memory file).\n- A lambda passed to a generic formal (`applyT\u003cT\u003e(f:T-\u003eT-\u003eInt, x:T, y:T)`)\n  has its parameters typed T, so `(a, b) -\u003e a - b` computes on erased slots\n  and `x \u003c y ? -1 : 1` on Strings compares pointers; the hint should\n  substitute T from the sibling arguments.\n- A method declared BELOW its caller with an inferred return type still\n  decays to Dynamic at the call (`cat(\"x\") != \"ax\"`). Lowering callee-first\n  (tried, reverted) made `makeExpr` return `Void-\u003eDynamic`, which boxed the\n  closure at `var e1:Dynamic = makeExpr(l)`, and Template's `::if (n \u003e 2)::`\n  took neither branch; the function-unbox at Let/return above is the first\n  half of what it needs.\n- haxe.Template `::if isMale::` with a false field prints the if branch on\n  HEAD as well (Issue8560 is WRONG for it); a renamed verbatim copy of\n  Template.hx in the user module runs `run(expr)` without ever reaching\n  OpIf, so the copy is not a faithful reproduction.\n- `Array\u003cFloat\u003e.map(f)`: the runtime calls the closure as `(env, i64) -\u003e\n  i64`, so a `(env, f64) -\u003e f64` lambda reads an integer register.","files":null}]}